---
// The Terms of Use (the Study Desk's step 4j; BVJ answered its decisions on
// 2026-11-25): one agreement for the website, the apps, and the optional
// account, written as plainly as the privacy page. Claude drafted it and it
// is not legal advice; whether a lawyer reads it is BVJ's call, and the
// parts worth a lawyer's time are listed under step 4j in STUDY-DESK.md.
//
// What BVJ decided: accounts are for 13 and older, or older where a
// country's law sets a higher age (decision 1); the page is live before
// accounts open, saying so (decision 2); the address is /terms/, in the
// footer beside Privacy (decision 3); the Liberating Scripture Collective is
// the party (decision 4); 90 days' notice before the account service ends
// (decision 5); Oregon law governs (LSC's home state, decision 8), and BVJ
// approved "The law that applies" as drafted from the research, with the
// courts "in Oregon" and no county.
//
// AT LAUNCH, "Accounts are not open yet" comes out of "Accounts" and the
// effective date moves (src/scripts/desk/account/CLAUDE.md, rule 11's list).
// The license at /read#license-terms, not this page, governs copying the
// translation; keep the two from contradicting each other.
//
// The privacy page's document column, used as it is (as /delete-account/
// does). Nothing is added to that stylesheet for this page.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../styles/pages/privacy.css";
import "../styles/v2/privacy.css";
---

<Layout
  title="Terms of Use | Liberation and Inclusion Translation"
  description="The LIT Bible terms of use: the agreement for the website, the apps, and the optional account that syncs your notebook."
  canonical="https://litbible.net/terms/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "WebPage",
      "name": "LIT Bible Terms of Use",
      "url": "https://litbible.net/terms/",
      "dateModified": "2026-11-25",
      "description": "The LIT Bible terms of use: the agreement for the website, the apps, and the optional account that syncs your notebook.",
      "isPartOf": {
        "@type": "WebSite",
        "name": "LIT Bible",
        "url": "https://litbible.net"
      },
      "publisher": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "LIT Bible",
        "url": "https://litbible.net"
      }
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <article class="privacy-page v2-frame" aria-labelledby="terms-title">

    <header class="privacy-header v2-frame__head v2-bar">
      <div class="privacy-header__inner">
        <h1 id="terms-title" class="privacy-title">Terms of Use</h1>
        <p class="privacy-effective">
          Effective date: <time datetime="2026-11-25">November 25, 2026</time>
        </p>
        <p class="privacy-lede">
          These terms are the agreement between you and the Liberating
          Scripture Collective for using the LIT Bible website (litbible.net),
          the LIT Bible apps for iOS and Android, and the optional LIT Bible
          account. We have tried to write them as plainly as the rest of the
          site. Our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> says what we collect
          and keep.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="privacy-toc v2-frame__toc" data-v2 data-pagefind-ignore aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="v2-label">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#terms-who">Who we are</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-reading">Reading and sharing the LIT</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-notebook">Your notebook is yours</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-accounts">Accounts</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-fair">Using the service fairly</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-ends">If the service changes or ends</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-promises">No promises beyond these</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-others">Other services</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-law">The law that applies</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-changes">Changes to these terms</a></li>
        <li><a href="#terms-contact">Questions</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="privacy-body v2-frame__text">

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-who">
        <h2 id="terms-who" class="v2-rule">Who we are</h2>
        <p>
          The LIT Bible is a project of the Liberating Scripture Collective
          (“we”), a 501(c)(3) nonprofit. The website, the apps, and the
          account are free. There are no ads and nothing to buy. Donations are
          welcome and never required.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-reading">
        <h2 id="terms-reading" class="v2-rule">Reading and sharing the LIT</h2>
        <p>
          Reading the LIT needs no account and has no age limit. The LIT text
          and footnotes are licensed under CC BY-NC-ND 4.0, with further
          permissions for church, study, and commercial use set out under
          <a href="/read#license-terms">License and reuse</a>. Those terms,
          not these, say how you may copy, print, and share the translation;
          the book introductions, glossary, and articles are under the same
          license. Material by others, such as the Greek text and its
          lexicons, is used under its own terms, named where it appears.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-notebook">
        <h2 id="terms-notebook" class="v2-rule">Your notebook is yours</h2>
        <p>
          What you write in the Study Desk or the apps (notes, highlights,
          bookmarks, sheets, and collections) belongs to you, and we claim no
          rights to it. Without an account it stays on your device. With an
          account, we keep a locked copy only so that your devices can sync
          it; we can’t read it, and we don’t use it for anything else. A sheet
          you share travels in the link itself, not through us, so what you
          share and with whom is up to you.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-accounts">
        <h2 id="terms-accounts" class="v2-rule">Accounts</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>An account is optional.</strong> You need one only to sync
            your notebook between devices. Accounts are not open yet; these
            terms apply to them when they do, and to anyone testing them now.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You must be at least 13 to create an account,</strong> or
            older if the law where you live sets a higher age for using an
            online service without a parent’s permission.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>An account is for one person.</strong> Keep your passkey,
            your devices, and your recovery code safe: anyone who has them can
            open your notebook.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>We can’t open your notebook, so we can’t recover
            it.</strong> If you lose every device and have neither a passkey
            that opens it nor your recovery code, no one can get the synced
            copy back, including us. A backup file, or a notebook still on one
            of your devices, is not affected.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You can stop at any time:</strong> stop syncing on a
            computer, sign out, or delete your account (see
            <a href="/delete-account/">Delete your account</a>). A request to
            delete made from a sign-in that hasn’t opened your notebook waits
            seven days, during which any of your devices can cancel it.
            Deleting can’t be undone.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-fair">
        <h2 id="terms-fair" class="v2-rule">Using the service fairly</h2>
        <p>
          Please don’t try to get into anyone else’s account or notebook;
          interfere with the website, the apps, or the account service, or
          overload them; create accounts by automated means; or use the
          account service to store anything but a notebook kept by the LIT
          Bible apps or website. The service limits how often a device may
          ask, and slows one that asks too often. We may suspend or close an
          account used in these ways. Since we can’t read notebooks, we would
          decide only from how the service is being used.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-ends">
        <h2 id="terms-ends" class="v2-rule">If the service changes or ends</h2>
        <p>
          We may change the website, the apps, and the account service, and
          add or remove features. If we decide to end the account service, we
          will say so on the website and in the apps at least 90 days before
          it ends, unless a security problem or a law requires us to act
          sooner. Your notebook stays on each of your devices, where you can
          keep using it and save it to a file. Since we keep no email address,
          the website and the apps are where we can tell you.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-promises">
        <h2 id="terms-promises" class="v2-rule">No promises beyond these</h2>
        <p>
          We offer the LIT Bible, the apps, and the account freely and as they
          are. We work to keep your notebook safe and in step, but we can’t
          promise that the website, the apps, or the account will always be
          available or free of errors, or that nothing will ever be lost. Keep
          a backup of anything you wouldn’t want to lose. To the fullest
          extent the law allows, the Liberating Scripture Collective and its
          volunteers and contributors are not liable for lost data or for any
          indirect or consequential damages from your use of the website, the
          apps, or the account. Some places don’t allow these limits, so they
          may not all apply to you.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-others">
        <h2 id="terms-others" class="v2-rule">Other services</h2>
        <p>
          Signing in with Apple or Google, a password manager that keeps your
          passkey, the App Store and Google Play, Give Lively (donations), and
          Brevo (the mailing list) are run by others under their own terms. If
          you got an app from the App Store or Google Play, that store’s terms
          apply to it too.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-law">
        <h2 id="terms-law" class="v2-rule">The law that applies</h2>
        <p>
          The Liberating Scripture Collective is based in Oregon. These terms
          are governed by the laws of the State of Oregon and of the United
          States, without regard to rules that would apply another place’s
          law. A dispute about them is for the state or federal courts in
          Oregon, unless the law where you live lets you bring it there.
          Nothing in these terms takes away a right that the law where you
          live gives you and doesn’t let an agreement change.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-changes">
        <h2 id="terms-changes" class="v2-rule">Changes to these terms</h2>
        <p>
          If we change these terms in a way that matters, we will update the
          effective date, say what changed at the top of this page, and note
          it in the apps. Using the account after a change takes effect means
          you accept the new terms; if you don’t, you can delete your account
          at any time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="terms-contact">
        <h2 id="terms-contact" class="v2-rule">Questions</h2>
        <p>
          Write to us at <a href="/contact/">litbible.net/contact</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>

  <script>
    // Site 2.0: marks the contents rail's current section (only with the
    // restyle's stamp; see the module's header).
    import "../scripts/v2-frame.js";
  </script>
</Layout>
